<body>
  <main class="container">

    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <div class="meta">
      {author} · {yearDisplay} · {rating} · Review not completed
    </div>

    <p class="review-complete">
  Serial killers occupy a peculiar place in the public imagination. Unlike
  crimes of passion or desperation, serial murder often involves patience,
  preparation, and the repeated pursuit of victims for motives that are
  frequently sadistic or sexual. The combination of careful planning and
  seemingly irrational motives has made serial killers a source of enduring
  fascination.
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  Few cases illustrate this puzzle better than that of
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israel_Keyes">Israel Keyes</a>. For
  more than a decade, he committed murders across the United States while
  largely avoiding suspicion, ultimately being apprehended not through a
  sophisticated investigation but because investigators traced a stolen credit
  card he used after one of his crimes.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Keyes was arrested in March 2012 after using
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samantha_Koenig">Samantha Koenig's</a>
    debit card following her murder. Investigators tracked ATM withdrawals and
    purchases across several states before locating him in Texas.
  </span>
  How could a killer remain undetected for so long, and did his success stem
  from extraordinary skill, institutional weaknesses, or simple luck?
  <i>American Predator</i> attempts to answer that question through a detailed
  reconstruction of Keyes' crimes and the investigation that finally ended
  them.
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  Keyes MO did not rely on a fixed victim type, or location for disposal and
  murder, with limits preventing investigators from linking cases through
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victimology">victimology</a>.
  <span class="sidenote">
    <b>Victimology</b> studies the characteristics and lifestyles of victims.
    Many serial murder investigations begin by noticing similarities among
    victims. Keyes intentionally selected victims with little connection to one
    another, making linkage far more difficult.
  </span>
  This reduced the potential contact between victims, witnesses, and Keyes.
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  He also left thousands of miles between him and his victims obscuring his
  travel patterns: flying to distant cities before renting cars and driving
  thousands of miles which made it difficult to connect him to his murders.
  <span class="sidenote">
    The FBI eventually reconstructed many of these trips using airline records,
    rental-car agreements, bank withdrawals, and surveillance footage after his
    arrest.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  Carefully planning his murders, he avoided bringing his cell-phone or removed
  the battery and used only paper maps to leave virtually no electronic
  paper-trails.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Most of Keyes' murders occurred before smartphones became ubiquitous.
    Removing a battery from many phones of the 2000s effectively prevented
    cellular location tracking. Today, this technique would generally not work
    because most smartphones have sealed batteries and numerous background
    location services.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  He buried caches (murder-kits) long ahead of time throughout the United
  States allowing him to have a clean purchase history and not possess his
  tools at any time.
  <span class="sidenote">
    The FBI recovered several buried "kill kits" after Keyes' arrest. They
    commonly contained firearms, ammunition, restraints, cash, and tools.
    Several had reportedly remained underground for years before being used.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  The last paragraph contains elements you could find from any summary. But
  what else did Keyes do to avoid
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DNA_profiling">DNA evidence</a>,
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Closed-circuit_television">CCTV</a>,
  or body discovery?
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  Keyes wore thick leather gloves in order to avoid leaving fingerprints or any
  DNA.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Gloves reduce direct fingerprint deposition but can themselves transfer
    oils or biological material between surfaces. According to the book, Keyes
    wiped his weapons and gloves using bleach and Lysol after crimes in an
    attempt to minimize transferable evidence.
  </span>
  He had minimal body hair—
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maureen_Callahan_(journalist)"
    >McCallahan</a
  >
  goes as far as to hint that he got laser hair removal though this is probably
  one of the more speculative claims in the book.
  <span class="sidenote">
    There is no public confirmation that Keyes underwent laser hair removal.
    McCallahan infers the possibility because investigators recovered
    remarkably little hair evidence.
  </span>
  He wore layers and had minimal contact with the victim to avoid skin cell
  transfer.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Modern forensic investigations increasingly rely on
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trace_evidence">trace evidence</a>:
    microscopic hairs, fibers, skin cells, pollen, soil, and other tiny
    materials that can link a suspect to a victim or crime scene.
  </span>
</p>
<p class="review-complete">
  Keyes was nowhere near perfect; however, many of his murders relied on an
  element of luck. In his final murder of
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samantha_Koenig">Samantha Koenig</a>,
  she almost escaped and nearby passerby did not realize or help her before he
  caught her and forced her into his car at gunpoint and later claimed he
  didn’t know credit cards could be tracked.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Ironically, this mistake—not DNA, eyewitnesses, or criminal profiling—was
    ultimately what led investigators to Keyes. After Koenig's murder he used
    her debit card repeatedly, allowing investigators to follow a trail of ATM
    withdrawals across several states.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  In the
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_and_Lorraine_Currier">
    Currier murders
  </a>,
  Lorraine almost runs away before he chases her and tackles her down.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Bill and Lorraine Currier disappeared from their Vermont home in 2011.
    Their bodies have never been recovered despite extensive searches after
    Keyes confessed to the murders.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  The book however often presents an over-idealized version of Keyes
  emphasizing his meticulous planning over his shortcomings.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Throughout the investigation there are numerous examples of Keyes making
    mistakes: being observed by witnesses, abandoning plans, changing targets,
    and relying upon circumstances outside his control. Many crimes succeeded
    partly because no one connected isolated disappearances occurring hundreds
    or thousands of miles apart.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  His background also played a part in Keyes’s untraceability. Born to
  ex-Mormons who were distrustful of the government, he was not on virtually
  any government database.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Keyes' family eventually became associated with the
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_Identity">
      Christian Identity
    </a>
    movement, an extremist religious ideology emphasizing racial separatism.
    McCallahan argues that his isolated upbringing also meant relatively few
    official records existed from his childhood.
  </span>
  He wasn’t even in the
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_Security_(United_States)">
    Social Security
  </a>
  database until he enlisted in the army.
  <span class="sidenote">
    A Social Security record is not itself a criminal database. Rather, it
    serves as one of many government records investigators can later use to
    reconstruct a person's history.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  McCallahan's prose is sparse and without ornamentation or frills which is
  perfect for a book about a methodical killer. The book begins how Keyes would
  tell stories with the end of his murderous career before interspersing
  interviews, flashbacks, and reconstructions of his murders.
  <span class="sidenote">
    The structure mirrors many of the FBI interviews with Keyes, who often
    preferred discussing crimes indirectly or out of chronological order rather
    than providing investigators with a complete confession.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  The problem is that much relies on Keyes’s account as his MO was difficult to
  back up or check--- even his murder count is debated but McCallahan does her
  best to introduce evidence where possible: such as Keyes’ correct
  recollection of the floorplan and layout during the Currier murders.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Keyes frequently refused to identify victims unless certain conditions were
    met, and committed suicide in prison before investigators could verify many
    of his claims.
  </span>
  <span class="sidenote">
    His accurate recollection of the Currier home—including its interior
    layout—was one reason investigators considered at least portions of his
    confession credible despite the lack of physical evidence.
  </span>
  <span class="sidenote">
    The exact number of murders remains unknown. Keyes admitted to fewer than a
    dozen killings, while the FBI believes there were likely additional victims
    that may never be identified.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-complete">
  True crime is often sensationalized but McCallahan’s account reads serious
  and descriptive of all the right facts.
  <span class="sidenote">
    Unlike many popular true-crime books, <i>American Predator</i> spends
    relatively little time speculating about Keyes' psychology and instead
    emphasizes investigative records, FBI interviews, and documented evidence.
  </span>
</p>

<p class="review-incomplete">
  [WIP]
</p>

  </main>
